//? Title: Bayesian data analysis examples
//? Exports: both
//? Lang: en-GB

\abstract(
Executable C++ examples of Bayesian modelling in datamunge, from exact
conjugate updates through generalized linear models, hierarchical models
and marginalization.
)

> Start here

- [Two samples|../group-comparisons/two_samples.mepml] and [k samples|../group-comparisons/k_samples.mepml] derive posterior contrasts for a continuous response grouped by a categorical predictor.
- [Simple regression|../linear-regression/simple_regression.mepml] and [multiple regression|../linear-regression/multiple_regression.mepml] compare Gaussian posterior coefficient estimates with ordinary least squares.
- [Beta-binomial|../conjugacy/beta_binomial.mepml] and [Gamma-Poisson|../conjugacy/gamma_poisson.mepml] derive exact posterior updates and predictive probabilities.
- [Eight schools|../hierarchical/eight_schools.mepml] compares noncentered NUTS and hierarchical Normal quadrature. [Hierarchical logistic regression|../hierarchical/logistic_pooling.mepml] shows partial pooling for binary outcomes.

- [Bayesian additive regression trees|../regression-trees/bart_regression.mepml] fits a nonlinear interaction and compares mean and observation intervals.

> Generalized linear and count models

- [Grouped binomial|../glm/binomial.mepml] fits a logit link to counts with known numbers of trials.
- [Poisson|../glm/poisson.mepml] and [negative binomial|../glm/negative_binomial.mepml] fit log means under different variance assumptions.
- [Gamma|../glm/gamma.mepml] fits positive continuous outcomes with a log mean.
- [Zero-inflated Poisson|../glm/zero_inflated_poisson.mepml] and [Poisson hurdle|../glm/poisson_hurdle.mepml] model excess zeros in different ways.
- [Categorical links|../glm/categorical_links.mepml] works through binary probit, ordered logit and multinomial logit.
- [Poisson replication|../predictive-checks/poisson_replication.mepml] uses posterior predictive checks to assess excess zeros and an extreme maximum.

> Stan 2.19 chapter coverage

The chapter numbers follow [Part 1 of the Stan User’s Guide 2.19|https://mc-stan.org/docs/2_19/stan-users-guide-2_19.pdf].
Each chapter has a worked datamunge example. Several chapters contain variants
beyond the examples linked here.

1. Regression. [Gaussian linear regression|../linear-regression/multiple_regression.mepml], [Student-t errors|../linear-regression/robust_regression.mepml] [categorical links|../glm/categorical_links.mepml], [hierarchical logistic regression|../hierarchical/logistic_pooling.mepml] and [posterior predictive checking|../predictive-checks/poisson_replication.mepml].
2. Time series. [AR(1)|../time-series/ar1.mepml] and a [hidden Markov model|../time-series/hidden_markov.mepml].
3. Missing data. [Ignorable missing responses|../data-quality/missing_responses.mepml].
4. Truncated or censored data. [Left truncation|../data-quality/left_truncation.mepml] and [right censoring|../data-quality/right_censoring.mepml].
5. Finite mixtures. [Gaussian mixtures|../mixtures/gaussian_clustering.mepml], [zero inflation|../glm/zero_inflated_poisson.mepml] and [hurdles|../glm/poisson_hurdle.mepml].
6. Measurement error and meta-analysis. [Predictor measurement error|../data-quality/measurement_error.mepml] and [eight schools|../hierarchical/eight_schools.mepml].
7. Latent discrete parameters. [Poisson change point|../latent-variables/poisson_changepoint.mepml] and [mark–recapture|../latent-variables/mark_recapture.mepml].
8. Sparse and ragged data. [Ragged groups|../data-structures/ragged_groups.mepml].
9. Clustering. [Gaussian mixture assignments|../mixtures/gaussian_clustering.mepml].
10. Gaussian processes. [Posterior smoothing|../gaussian-processes/gp_smoothing.mepml].
11. Directions and rotations. [Circular location|../directional/circular_location.mepml].
12. Algebraic equations. [Implicit equilibrium|../mechanistic/implicit_equilibrium.mepml].
13. Ordinary differential equations. [Numerical decay model|../mechanistic/exponential_decay.mepml].
14. One-dimensional integration. [Integrated Poisson exposure|../mechanistic/integrated_exposure.mepml].

> Data and fitted values

[Eight schools|../hierarchical/eight_schools.mepml] uses published treatment
effect estimates and reported standard errors. The conjugate reports generate
their own observations with fixed seeds and check the exact updates against
the likelihood evaluated on those observations. The other introductory
analyses use small synthetic datasets embedded in the programs. Fit panels show
posterior expected responses, predictive distributions, or latent quantities
alongside the observations. [Nested sampling|../evidence/nested_sampling_analysis.mepml]
also fits a set of synthetic calibration readings.

> Run the reports

Use a report toolchain that provides C++23, mep-mepml and the datamunge
pkg-config entry. From the repository root, run an individual report with
`just docs-mepml-run docs/mepml/bayes/glm/poisson.mepml`. Each program
writes an SVG into its report’s local `build/` directory. The runner
copies that figure to the path referenced by the report.
